export const DE_C2_S04_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-die-kaiserurkunde": [
    `Every bottle that leaves the Sankt Quirin abbey brewery in Hohenried carries a charter on its label. You can hardly make it out, it has shrunk to the size of a postage stamp, but everyone in the district knows what it says: that in the year 1347 Emperor Ludwig the Bavarian granted the monks of Sankt Quirin the water of the Quirin spring for brewing, "for all eternity". The original hangs in the cloister behind armoured glass, and the tourists photograph it before ordering their dark beer in the brewery tavern. I had come to Hohenried in the spring to catalogue the abbey archive, work you can safely imagine as quiet: dust, cardboard boxes, coffee from a thermos and now and then an account book from the eighteenth century that gives you disproportionate joy.`,
    `In June I opened an unlabelled box that had been standing behind a shelf of parish newsletters. In it were forty or fifty sheets, closely written in a Gothic chancery script that I recognised at once, because I had seen it every morning in the cloister for three months. It was always the same sentence, "We, Ludwig, by the grace of God Roman Emperor", again and again, with growing confidence, as if someone had been learning to write all over again. The backs were parish newsletters from 1953. At the very bottom of the box lay a slip of paper with a recipe: oak galls, iron vitriol, gum arabic. Ink as it was mixed in the Middle Ages. Below it, in pencil, two letters: P. A.`,
    `The files I had come to catalogue did the rest. In 1953 a big Munich brewery had laid claim to the Quirin spring, citing the secularisation of 1803, when the Bavarian state had dissolved the monasteries and seized their property; the monks, who had only returned decades later, had, so the plaintiffs argued, no documented right to the water. The case dragged on, the abbey faced ruin, and eighty jobs in the village with it. In March 1954 the abbey archivist, Father Anselm Huber, found a charter of Ludwig the Bavarian during building work in a bricked-up niche in the cloister. The court examined it, marvelled and dismissed the claim.`,
    `I carried the box to the abbot. Father Gregor is a small, friendly man with the face of an accountant and the humour of a cabaret artist, and he listened to me without interrupting. Then he stood up, fetched two glasses and a bottle of dark beer, poured, and said: "You want to know whether I knew." He had known, since 2011. Before his death Father Anselm had deposited a sealed envelope in the abbey chronicle, "to be opened by my next successor but one as archivist, or by the abbot, should he be more curious". The abbot had been more curious.`,
    `He gave me the letter to read. In it Anselm wrote that he had invented nothing. The monks had used the water of the spring for six hundred years, it was in every account book, and there had been a charter, it was even mentioned in an inventory of 1612; it had simply been loaded onto a cart to Munich with everything else in 1803 and had never arrived. "I did not forge the truth," he wrote, "only its paper. Whether God makes a distinction here, I do not know. The court would not have made one."`,
    `"And now?" I asked. The abbot shrugged. Legally, he said, nothing was at stake any more; the water rights had long been secured by centuries of use, he had made enquiries to be on the safe side. All that was at stake was the vanity of a monastery, and that could stand a penance. "You're the scholar. A forgery remains a forgery, even if it was well meant, or especially then. Write what you found." I asked him whether he was saying that as abbot or as brewery owner. "As both," he said. "In Bavaria the two aren't mutually exclusive."`,
    `My article appeared in the autumn in a specialist journal that is normally read by two hundred people. Three days later a tabloid ran the headline "Monk forged emperor's charter for beer", and for two weeks Hohenried was the most famous place in Upper Bavaria. I received letters calling me someone who fouls her own nest, and others that wanted Father Anselm canonised. The cloister was fuller than ever.`,
    `The brewery reacted as you might expect of a brewery that has existed for six hundred years. In December a new beer came onto the market, an honest dark beer called "Father Anselm's Forgery", with the charter on the label and beneath it, in Gothic script: "False, but well meant". It sold out within a week. The abbot sent me the first crate with a card: "For the truth, which cost us dearly and sells astonishingly well." Today the practice sheets lie in a display case next to the charter. In the archive catalogue I have recorded them under a category that did not exist before: "Forgery, genuine".`,
  ],
  "de-heiterkeit-im-ganzen-hause": [
    `For twenty-two years I have sat in the plenary chamber of the German Bundestag at the little table below the lectern and written down what is said. Everything: the speeches, the questions, the heckling, the ringing of the bell. We parliamentary stenographers take turns every ten minutes, so that the hand doesn't tire and the head doesn't start thinking along, which in this profession is a danger. What we write becomes the plenary record, and the plenary record is, as our longest-serving colleague likes to say, the only thing in this house that is guaranteed to be passed for eternity.`,
    `Our craft has a language of its own, and it is subtler than you'd think. When one parliamentary group laughs at another, we write "Lachen", laughter. But when the house laughs at something without anyone laughing at anyone else, we write "Heiterkeit", amusement. The difference is the one between mockery and pleasure, and getting it right in a split second is, with all due modesty, an art. "Amusement throughout the house" is rare. In twenty-two years I have written it eleven times.`,
    `Johanna Seidel came to us from the Saxon state parliament three years ago, and since then she has relieved me, or I her, depending on the rota. The changeover follows a silent ritual: one gets up, the other sits down, you nod to each other, and in between two words are spoken, namely "Bitte" and "Danke". Johanna and I have not said more to each other than that in three years, apart from one Christmas party at which I held forth on comma placement in committee recommendations until she politely escaped to the buffet. But I read her records. She distinguishes "objection" from "commotion" with a sureness I envy, and once she recorded an interjection in such a way that the member who had made it asked for a correction, because in the record he sounded cleverer than he had meant to. Some people fall in love with eyes. I fell in love with brackets.`,
    `In November the house sat until late into the night. At twenty to two the second and third readings of a bill amending the Federal Forest Act were on the agenda, thirty-one members were still in the chamber, and at the lectern stood Hinrich Ohlsen from Schleswig-Holstein, thirty-two years in the Bundestag, giving his last speech that evening. He spoke about beeches and bark beetles, and then, when the red lamp on the lectern was already flashing, he turned to the stenographers. He thanked them, he said, for thirty-two years of patience, "for you write down everything we say, and mercifully not everything we mean". I wrote: "(Amusement)".`,
    `Then he turned round to me. "And to you, Herr Brenner, I would like to give the following parting advice. If you would finally tell your colleague Frau Seidel what this house has known for three years, then tonight we would at least have passed one resolution that lasts." I wrote. In my life I have taken down budget debates, votes of no confidence and a member who spoke in rhyme; none of it was as hard as writing my own name into an official document while thirty-one representatives of the people looked at me. The president rang the bell. "Honourable member, the stenographers are not the subject of the debate." "Not yet, Madam President," said Ohlsen.`,
    `At that moment my turn was over. I got up, and Johanna sat down, and we looked at each other for longer than the ritual allows. "Bitte," I said, because nothing else occurred to me. She took the pen, wrote something and then said, loudly and clearly, into the silence of the almost empty chamber: "Ja."`,
    `The next morning the provisional record lay on my desk. I read the passage so often that my colleagues began to worry. After Ohlsen's sentence it said, in Johanna's impeccable order: "(Call: Yes!)", and below it: "(Amusement throughout the house – applause)". Who had called out was not noted. That is customary with calls from the floor when the caller cannot be identified beyond doubt, and Johanna, this much is certain, has never noted anything she could not identify beyond doubt.`,
    `We married in May. Hinrich Ohlsen gave a speech that was considerably longer than the permitted speaking time, and out of old habit I took it down in shorthand on a napkin. At the end I noted: "(Lively applause)". In terms of the record, it was an understatement. But there is no step up from "amusement throughout the house", and the twelfth time belonged to Johanna anyway.`,
  ],
  "de-im-lerche": [
    `For thirty-five years I did not apply to see my file. Friends from the church study group, who had done it right away in 1992, told of marriages that had broken up over it, and of colleagues they no longer said hello to in the corner shop afterwards. I didn't want to know who. To be honest, I wanted to be able to go on pretending I didn't know, and over time this not-knowing became a kind of piece of furniture you get used to. Only after my husband had died and my daughter said that one day she would like to know what sort of life her mother had led did I put in the application. Last spring I sat in the reading room of the archive in Rostock in front of three hundred and twelve pages.`,
    `Most of it was so tedious that I almost felt insulted. For years a lieutenant had noted when I unlocked the city library in the Südstadt, when I locked it, and which books I carried home. Then came the reports by unofficial collaborator "Lerche", the lark: forty-one of them, from 1984 to 1988, typewritten, two or three pages each. I read them holding my breath, ready for betrayal. I found something else. "W. writes poems about seagulls, autumn leaves and the sea," it said. "No content hostile to the state is discernible. W. is politically insignificant. No measures are recommended."`,
    `Lerche summarised my poems, and with a care that alarmed me, because I have never experienced it from any reviewer. She knew poems I had only read aloud in the study group, to six or seven people, and she quoted them correctly, except in one place. The report from May 1986 said: "According to W., the word 'wall' in the poem of 3 May refers to the harbour wall in Warnemünde." That wasn't true. The poem was about a quite different wall, and everyone who had heard it knew that. Next to it, in the handwriting of the handling officer, in green ballpoint: "Informant delivers extensively but unproductively. Continuation questionable." Lerche went on delivering anyway, for four years, as unproductive as she was reliable, and it was only in the reading room that I grasped that a report containing nothing occupied the place where another, more zealous report would otherwise have lain.`,
    `In the report from February 1986 I found the sentence that settled everything. "W. cooks solyanka on Thursdays; the smell is noticeable throughout the stairwell." Whoever knew that had not only sat in the study group. Whoever knew that had stood in the stairwell. In 1986 four households lived in our entrance. Two are dead, one moved to Bavaria after the Wende. The fourth is Frau Gerda Pagel, ground floor left, ninety-three years old, to whom I have carried a bowl of solyanka downstairs every Thursday for twelve years, because she can no longer cook herself.`,
    `The friendly member of staff in the reading room explained that I could apply to have the code name decoded; that would take a few months. I thanked her and said it wasn't necessary. I took the tram home, sat down in the kitchen and stayed there until it was dark. I didn't sleep that night, but not out of anger. I remembered, I admit, every single time Gerda Pagel had rung my doorbell in the eighties to borrow sugar, and I wondered how much sugar a person actually needs.`,
    `On Thursday I cooked solyanka and carried it downstairs. Gerda sat at the kitchen table as always, the radio was on, and she ate, as always, slowly and with great attention. "Too much lemon," she said, as always. I said I had read my file. She put down her spoon. Outside the tram went past. "And?" she asked at last. "You summarised my poems very well," I said. She looked at her plate for a long time. "The one with the harbour wall," she said, "was the best."`,
    `She didn't explain any more than that, and I didn't ask. She said only one more sentence, so quietly that I almost missed it: back then they had had her boy, over his university place, and she had thought that if somebody was going to write about me, it had better be her. She didn't ask for forgiveness. I think she considered that a presumption, as if she would be disposing of something that wasn't hers to dispose of.`,
    `"Thank you," I said. She looked up. "Whatever for?" "For the boredom," I said. Then Gerda Pagel laughed, for the first time that afternoon, a dry, surprised laugh, and pushed the empty plate towards me. "That was the hardest part," she said. "Because you weren't boring at all." The following Thursday I brought her solyanka again. With just as much lemon as always.`,
  ],
  "de-der-schwarze-stein": [
    `In the spring of 2001 I was working as a stonemason on the Frauenkirche building site, and anyone who lived in Dresden at the time will remember the sight: a light-coloured, new sandstone building growing upwards ring by ring, and in it, scattered like freckles, the black stones. They were the old ones, recovered from the mountain of rubble that had lain in the middle of the city for almost fifty years after the church collapsed two days after the air raid in February 1945. Each of these stones had been measured, photographed and numbered, and a computer had calculated where it had once sat. Wherever at all possible, we put it back in exactly that place.`,
    `One Tuesday in April an old woman stood at the site fence, small, in a dark blue coat, and asked for the person "in charge of the old stones". The foreman sent her to me, presumably because I was the youngest and he regarded visitors as a form of work you leave to the youngest. She introduced herself as Ilse Lehmann, born 1925, from Striesen. She was looking for a stone. It bore an inscription, scratched in, not large: "H. K. + I. L. 1944".`,
    `I explained to her, as kindly as I could, that we had around eight thousand recovered pieces and that inscriptions, unless they were baroque masons' marks, had hardly been systematically recorded. She nodded as if she had expected this answer, and didn't budge. I looked at her, then I looked at my watch, and then I did something no work instruction provided for: I took her into the site container, sat down at the archaeologists' computer and began to go through the photos of the pieces from the south portal.`,
    `I found it after two hours. Find number 2214, a block from the plinth next to the steps at the south portal, black like all the others, one corner chipped off. On the front, inconspicuous, as if scratched with a penknife: "H. K. + I. L. 1944". And below it, which she hadn't mentioned, a second, clumsier line: "I. L. 1946". I turned the screen towards her. She looked at the photo for a very long time and then said, without taking her eyes off it: "The second line is mine."`,
    `Hans Kühn had been an apprentice with a bookbinder in the Neustadt. In the summer of 1944 they had sat on these steps one Sunday afternoon, and he had scratched the letters into the stone with his penknife while she scolded him that you don't do that sort of thing to a church. In the autumn he was called up. In March 1946 the news came that he had already been killed in East Prussia in January 1945. By then the church had long been a heap of rubble, and Ilse Lehmann had gone there and searched among the chunks, for days, until she found her stone, barely ten metres from the spot where it had sat. She had scratched the second line beneath, with a nail, because she didn't own a knife.`,
    `"I went there every Sunday," she said. "For forty-seven years. Everyone thought I was mourning the church. I never contradicted anyone." Then, in 1993, they had begun to clear the mountain of rubble, stone by stone, with cranes and tarpaulins, and one Sunday her stone had disappeared. "Since then I haven't known where to go," she said. "I just wanted to know whether it was still there."`,
    `Afterwards I spoke to the site manager, a thorough Westphalian, who at first objected that a scrawl from 1944 was not part of the original baroque fabric and, strictly speaking, ought to be removed. I replied that with this church we were precisely not pretending that the year 1945 had never happened, otherwise we wouldn't have put the black stones in at all. He looked at his plan for a while and then said the stone would go in its place, front facing outwards, as was proper. In the autumn I set it myself: find number 2214, plinth, south portal, the inscription forty centimetres above the pavement.`,
    `Ilse Lehmann came to the consecration in October 2005, in the dark blue coat, and sat down on the new steps next to the old stone, as if she had kept a seat free. She died in 2011. I sometimes go there when I'm in the city. Among the tourists looking up at the dome you can hardly see the inscription, and that, I think, is what she would have wanted. You have to bend down to read it. But it is there, and you know where to go.`,
  ],
  "de-die-vorleserin": [
    `The advertisement hung on the noticeboard of the German department in Graz in the summer semester, handwritten in an old-fashioned, very upright script: "Reader wanted. July and August. Free board and lodging. Knowledge of nineteenth-century literature desirable, enthusiasm for it not required." Lena Pichler, in her third year and chronically broke, found the last sentence above all irresistible. Two weeks later she was sitting on the bus towards Leibnitz and then in the car of a housekeeper who didn't say a single word during the whole drive along the South Styrian Wine Road.`,
    `The old gentleman's name was Leopold Thurnegg, and in the village everyone still called him the Count, although there have been no titles of nobility in Austria since 1919 and he himself, as he stressed at their first afternoon snack, set no store by such nonsense. He had been blind for eleven years. He sat in an armchair in the library, his hands on his stick, and had someone read to him every afternoon from three to six, with a break for coffee and apricot cake. He had a list. It began with Stifter's "Indian Summer".`,
    `Anyone who knows "Indian Summer" will understand what happened next. After two weeks and two hundred pages in which roses were lengthily tended, marble floors cleaned and feelings controlled for so long that you forgot they had ever existed, Lena began to add little things. First a thunderstorm. Then a dog that stole the young Heinrich's portfolio of drawings. Then, on a particularly sultry afternoon, a letter that Natalie secretly placed under a rose. The old gentleman sat motionless, as always, and merely said, whenever she paused: "Go on."`,
    `Over time she grew bolder. The list led via Grillparzer and Keller to Fontane, and with "Effi Briest" Lena couldn't bear it any longer. Even at school she hadn't wanted Effi to die, and now, in a manor house in southern Styria, with an audience of precisely one person, she had the power to prevent it for the first time. Her Effi left Kessin before the letters were found, moved to Berlin, learned photography and opened a studio in the Friedrichstraße, where, much to her parents' annoyance, she also took portraits of women without a chaperone. Lena read all this off the page without faltering, as if it were written there. She was proud of herself and a little ashamed, and sometimes she didn't know which outweighed the other.`,
    `On the last day of August, after the last chapter of her Effi, she shut the book and said she had something to confess. The old gentleman raised his hand. "Let me guess," he said. "In Fontane, Effi dies." And then, slowly and without hesitation, he recited the novel's last sentence, the one about the wide field, the way you recite a prayer you never learned and yet know.`,
    `His wife, he told her, had read him these books for forty years, every evening, the same ones again and again, because she felt that good books only began the third time round. He knew them all by heart, every comma in "Indian Summer", God help him. After her death he had hired readers, one after another: students, a retired teacher, an actress from Vienna. They had all read impeccably. And every time Effi had died, at exactly the same point, and he had noticed that he listened like someone standing at a grave. "You are the first," he said, "who has read me something I didn't know in eleven years. The dog, by the way, was excellent. Stifter could have used him."`,
    `Lena asked why he hadn't said anything. He thought about it. "Because otherwise you would have stopped," he said. "And because I wanted to know how far you would go. Very far, as it turned out. I liked the Friedrichstraße." Then he asked her to come back the following summer. On one condition, however: she was to bring nothing more from his library, only her own work. "I'm too old for books whose endings I know," he said. "Life has read me enough of those already."`,
    `She came back the following summer, and the summer after that, each time with a stack of written pages she had worked on all year. Leopold Thurnegg died in the fourth winter. Lena's first novel, which came out the spring after, is about a woman photographer in Berlin. It is dedicated to a man who never read a line of it and yet knew every word before it was printed: "For L. T., who always said 'Go on'."`,
  ],
};

